= Presupposition — Technical =

== Projection problem ==
Let trigger T introduce presupposition p inside a larger expression F(T).

The central problem is to predict when p projects to the global discourse level, is filtered by local information, is accommodated, or is canceled/blocked.

Simple truth-functional composition does not capture these patterns.

== Dynamic treatment ==
Dynamic frameworks model sentences as update functions on contexts.

A presupposition can be treated as a definedness condition:

<pre>
C + sentence is defined only if C satisfies p
</pre>

Accommodation modifies C when the condition is not already met and the update is otherwise acceptable.

== Local and global accommodation ==
Embedding under negation, conditionals, attitude predicates, and quantifiers motivates distinctions among local, intermediate, and global accommodation.

Competing theories balance semantic constraints, discourse coherence, processing cost, and pragmatic reasoning differently.

== Diagnostics and limits ==
Persistence under negation, projection behavior, backgrounding, and accommodation are useful diagnostics, but no single test supplies a universal category boundary.

Experimental work shows that trigger type, discourse context, prosody, and speaker assumptions affect interpretation.

